          "sourceRoman": "akṣakanō, athavā nīnēnō yārō onta svistāravāgi hēḷōṇtandāga, āga kṛṣṇa hēḷtā iddāne, ananta andare nānē. nā ella sarvavācya pratipādyakanāgiratakkantavanu nānē āgiddēne. adakkōskara ananta yāru annō vicāradalli saṃśaya bēḍa. viśvarūpiyāgiratakkantaha ananta nāmaka paramātma nānē. idaralli yāva saṃśayavū illa anta hēḷiddāne. ananta padmanābha embatakkantavanu yāru embudannu tiḷidantaha dharmarāja kṛṣṇanna kuritu hēḷtā iddāne. kṛṣṇa, ananta vratada mahimeyannu mattu adannu māḍuvantaha vidhānavannu tiḷisu. adarinda ēnu puṇya baruttāyide, phala entādu baruttāyide, ī vratadalli yāva dēva, yāva yāva dēvara pūje vihitavāgide, mattu pūrvadalli idannu yāru ācaraṇe māḍiddāre, bhūlōkadalli yāru prakaṭa māḍiddāre, ellavannū kūḍa nanage hēḷabēku antandāga kṛṣṇa ondu itihāsavannu hēḷtā iddāne. nōḍappa, pūrvadalli, andare kṛtayugadalli sumantu emba brāhmaṇa, sadbrāhmaṇanāgiratakkantaha avanu sundariyāgiddantaha dīkṣā embatakkanta kanyeyannu vēdōkta rītiyalli vivāha māḍikoṇḍidda. swalpa kālānantara aparimita strī lakṣaṇagaḷinda tumbiratakkanta avaḷu magaḷu huṭṭiddāḷe. avaḷige śīle anta nāmakaraṇa māḍiddāre. swalpa divasa āda nantara dharmāsaktanāgiratakkantaha sumantu mattobbaḷannū kūḍa karkase embatakkanta innobbaḷannū kūḍa vivāha māḍikoṇḍiddāve. avaḷu tumba krūra svabhāvadavaḷāgiddāḷe. yāvāgalū kūḍa jagaḷa āḍtā iddaḷu. modalē heṇḍatiya magaḷādantaha śīleyannu kaṇḍanta sumantu, avaḷu yauvanakke bandāga avaḷige yōgyanādantaha varanannu huḍukuttidda. antā sandarbhadalli vēdajñanādantaha kauṇḍinya emba takkanta ṛṣivarya kanyārthiyāgi banda. sumantu bahaḷa santōṣinda oppi, ā śīleyannu kauṇḍinyanige koṭṭu lagna māḍiddāne. tanna patniyādantaha karkaśege ā sumantu brāhmaṇa, nōḍu, vivāha āda mēle magaḷu aḷeyanige baḷuvaḷi rūpavāgi ēnādarū uttamavāgirtakkanta vastuvannu koḍabēku, ēnādarū koḍu antandāga, modalē duṣṭa svabhāvadavaḷādantaha ā karkaśe alaṅkārada vastuvannella muccittu, oduvavarige tindulidantaha hiṭṭininda buttiyannu kaṭṭikoṭṭu, idannē tegedukoṇḍu manege hōgabēku anta hēḷiddāḷe. ādare sthiravāgirtakkanta manassiddantaha kauṇḍinya mattu ā śīle ibbarū kūḍa adannē tegedukoṇḍu tamma āśramakke horaṭiddāre. nadī mukhanāgi horaṭantaha kauṇḍinya ā madhyāhna bhōjana samayadalli nadi daḍadalli caturdaśi dinavāgirtakkanta andu nārāyaṇanannu bhaktiyinda pūjistakkantaha kelavu strī samūhadavarannu kaṇḍantaha ā śīle ā heṇṇu makkaḷanna kuritu, nīvella ēnu pūje māḍtā iddīri? dayamāḍi nanagū kūḍa tiḷisabēku antandāga, ā heṇṇumakkaḷellarū saha, nīvu nāvu māḍtakkantaha pūjege ananta vrata anta karītā iddāre. ī vratadalli paramātma ananta emba takk antaha hesarininda pūjisalpaḍtā iddāne antandāga. ā kauṇḍinyana patniyāda śīle uttamavāgiratakkanta ī pūjeyannu nānu māḍtēne. ī vratavannu vidhivattāgi ācarisuvantaha kramavannu ēnēnu dāna koḍabēku adannū kūḍa vivaravāgi tiḷisi antandāga, ā heṇṇumakkaḷellarū, ā śīle, nōḍamma, ī vratavannu māḍatakkanta krama ī rītiyide antēḷi, sakramavāgiratakkanta ananta pūjā paddhatiyannu upadēśa māḍiddāre. nantara hadinālku gaṇṭugaḷuḷḷantaha dōravannu, anantara dāravannu avaḷige koṭṭu, ī dāravannu hiḍidukoṇḍu matte anantana pūjeyannu māḍu anta hēḷiddār. ā śīle tānū kūḍa bhāḷa santōṣavāgide, vratavannu māḍtīni antēḷi kaiyalli dāravannu dharisi bandiddāḷe. manege bandantaha kauṇḍinya tōḷinalliddantaha dāravannu kaṇḍiddāne. nantara avanu, śīleyē, ninna tōḷinalli ī hosa dāravannu ēnō kaṭṭiddīyalla, idu yātakke? nannannu vaśīkaraṇa māḍabēkō anta ēnādarū upāya māḍiddīyō, satyavannu hēḷu anta hēḷiddāne. āga ā śīle, yāva dēvana anugrahadinda namage iṣṭella sampattu bandidiyō, antaha ananta padmanābhana sannidhāna uḷḷantaha dōravannu nānu kaṭṭikoṇḍiddēne anta hēḷiddāḷe. ādare sampattininda madōnmattanāgiratakkantaha andhanādantaha kauṇḍinya anantanannu tiraskāra māḍi, ā dāravannu avaḷa kaiyinda kittu...",
          "english": "When someone—perhaps Akṣaka, or you, or somebody else—asked that it be explained at some length, Kṛṣṇa said, “Ananta means me. I am the one denoted and expounded by all words. Therefore, do not entertain any doubt about who Ananta is. I myself am the Paramātman named Ananta, who possesses the universal form. There is no doubt whatsoever about this,” he said. Having understood who the one called Ananta Padmanābha is, Dharmarāja addresses Kṛṣṇa: “Kṛṣṇa, explain the greatness of the Ananta Vrata and the procedure for performing it. What merit arises from it, what fruit results, which deity or deities are prescribed for worship in this vrata, who observed it formerly, and who made it known in the earthly realm—you must tell me everything.” When he asked this, Kṛṣṇa began to relate a sacred history. “Look, formerly, in the Kṛta Yuga, there was a brāhmaṇa named Sumantu. He was a virtuous brāhmaṇa and had married, according to the Vedic injunctions, a beautiful maiden named Dīkṣā. After some time, she, who was endowed with immeasurable feminine virtues, gave birth to a daughter. They named her Śīle. Some days later, Sumantu, who was devoted to dharma, also married another woman named Karkase. She was extremely cruel by nature and was always quarreling. When Śīle, the daughter of his first wife, reached youth, Sumantu sought a suitable bridegroom for her. At that time, the eminent sage Kauṇḍinya, who knew the Vedas, came seeking the maiden’s hand. Sumantu agreed with great joy, gave Śīle to Kauṇḍinya, and performed their marriage. The brāhmaṇa Sumantu then said to his wife Karkaśe, ‘Look, after the marriage, we should give our daughter and son-in-law some fine object as a parting gift. Give them something.’ But Karkaśe, already wicked by nature, hid all the ornamental objects. Using flour left over after others had eaten, she packed them some food and said that they must take only this and go home. Nevertheless, Kauṇḍinya, whose mind was steadfast, and Śīle took it and set out for their āśrama. As Kauṇḍinya traveled toward the river, at midday, the time for a meal, Śīle saw on the riverbank a group of women devoutly worshiping Nārāyaṇa on that day of Caturdaśī. Śīle addressed the women: ‘What pūjā are all of you performing? Please explain it to me as well.’ All the women replied, ‘The pūjā we are performing is called the Ananta Vrata. In this vrata, the Paramātman is worshiped under the name Ananta.’ Kauṇḍinya’s wife Śīle then said, ‘I will perform this excellent pūjā. Please explain in detail the proper procedure for observing this vrata and what donations must be given.’ The women said to Śīle, ‘Look, child, this is the procedure for performing the vrata,’ and duly instructed her in the prescribed Ananta Pūjā method. They then gave her a dora—a thread bearing fourteen knots—and afterward told her, ‘Take this thread and perform Ananta’s pūjā.’ Śīle was herself very happy. Saying, ‘I will perform the vrata,’ she wore the thread on her arm and returned. When Kauṇḍinya came home, he saw the thread on her arm. He then said, ‘Śīle, you have tied some new thread around your arm. What is it for? Have you devised some means to bring me under your control? Tell me the truth.’ Śīle replied, ‘By the grace of the deity from whom all this wealth has come to us, I have tied this dora in which Ananta Padmanābha is present.’ But Kauṇḍinya, blinded and intoxicated with pride because of his wealth, rejected Ananta and tore the thread from her hand..."
